WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Product Manager: Alright everyone, thanks for joining today's kickoff meeting for the Grocery Price Tracker project.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:12.000
Product Manager: So the main problem we're trying to solve is helping people save money on groceries by tracking prices across different stores.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.000
Stakeholder 1: Yeah, I mean, I shop at like three different stores and I never remember which one has the best price for milk or eggs.

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:24.000
Developer: So what's the core functionality we absolutely need for the MVP? What's the minimum viable version?

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:32.000
Product Manager: Good question. At minimum, users need to be able to add grocery items they buy regularly - things like milk, bread, bananas.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:39.000
Product Manager: Then they need to log prices when they shop. Like, I bought milk at Whole Foods for $5.99 on June 1st.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:46.000
UX Designer: And they'd want to see those prices compared, right? So they can quickly tell which store is cheapest for each item.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:53.000
Product Manager: Exactly. We should highlight the best deal - maybe color code it green for the lowest price, red for the highest.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:01:00.000
Stakeholder 2: Can we add categories? Like dairy, produce, meat? That would help organize things.

00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:07.000
Product Manager: Hmm, categories are nice but not essential for MVP. Users can just search by item name to start.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:13.000
Developer: So for MVP we're talking about CRUD operations on items and prices, basic comparison view?

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:21.000
Product Manager: Yes. Add, view, edit, delete items. Add, view, delete price entries. Compare prices across stores for each item.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:28.000
UX Designer: Should prices be editable? In case someone types the wrong amount?

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:34.000
Product Manager: Good catch. Yes, let's add edit functionality for prices too. Part of the MVP.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:41.000
Stakeholder 1: What about taking pictures of receipts? That would be so much easier than manually typing everything.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:48.000
Product Manager: Love that idea, but that's definitely post-MVP. Receipt OCR is complex and would slow us down initially.

00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:55.000
Developer: Agreed. We'd need image processing, text extraction, parsing logic... that's a whole project itself.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:02.000
Stakeholder 2: What about barcode scanning? You could scan items in the store and it auto-fills the name.

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:09.000
Product Manager: Also really cool for the future, but not MVP. Adds complexity with camera access, barcode libraries, product databases.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:16.000
UX Designer: Should we have price history graphs? Like a chart showing how milk prices have changed over time?

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:23.000
Product Manager: That's post-MVP too. For now, just showing the list of prices sorted by date is enough. Graphs can come later.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:30.000
Stakeholder 1: Can we set budget alerts? Like notify me if my grocery spending goes over $500 this month?

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:37.000
Product Manager: That's moving into budget tracking territory. Out of scope for this project - we're focused on price comparison, not expense tracking.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:44.000
Developer: What about user accounts? Do people need to sign up and log in?

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:51.000
Product Manager: For MVP, no. We can use local storage or in-memory database. User accounts are post-MVP.

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:58.000
Stakeholder 2: But then data isn't saved if they close the browser, right?

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:05.000
Developer: We could use browser localStorage to persist data client-side for now. Good enough for MVP without needing authentication.

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:12.000
Product Manager: Actually, let's use an in-memory database on the backend for simplicity. It's a learning project, data persistence can come later.

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:19.000
UX Designer: What about adding notes to price entries? Like "on sale" or "organic version"?

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:26.000
Product Manager: Yes, let's include that in MVP. Just an optional text field. Simple but useful.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:33.000
Stakeholder 1: Could we integrate with store loyalty programs to automatically import purchases?

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:40.000
Product Manager: Whoa, way out of scope! That would require API partnerships with every grocery chain. Definitely not happening.

00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:47.000
Developer: What about sharing shopping lists with family members?

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:54.000
Product Manager: That's a different feature entirely - collaboration and sharing. Out of scope. This is single-user price tracking only.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:04:01.000
Stakeholder 2: Can we show stores on a map? Like highlight which nearby store has the best prices?

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:08.000
Product Manager: Maps and geolocation... that's post-MVP. Cool feature but adds significant complexity with mapping APIs.

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:15.000
UX Designer: Should the app work on mobile? Or just desktop?

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:22.000
Product Manager: It should be responsive - works on both. Use mobile-first design but no native mobile app for MVP.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:29.000
Developer: So a responsive web app. No iOS/Android apps?

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:36.000
Product Manager: Correct. Native mobile apps are out of scope. Progressive Web App could be post-MVP though.

00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:43.000
Stakeholder 1: What if I want to track unit prices? Like price per ounce or price per pound?

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:50.000
Product Manager: Interesting. That's more advanced - calculating price per unit, handling different units. Post-MVP.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:57.000
UX Designer: Should we have a dashboard showing overall savings? Like "You saved $47 this month by shopping smart"?

00:04:57.500 --> 00:05:04.000
Product Manager: That's analytics territory. Out of scope for MVP. We're just comparing prices, not calculating theoretical savings.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:11.000
Developer: What about price predictions? Using AI to forecast when prices might drop?

00:05:11.500 --> 00:05:18.000
Product Manager: Machine learning and predictions? Way beyond MVP! That's like a version 3.0 feature. Out of scope entirely.

00:05:18.500 --> 00:05:25.000
Stakeholder 2: Can multiple people track the same items? Like crowdsource price data?

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:32.000
Product Manager: That's a community feature with data aggregation. Out of scope. This is single-user for MVP.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:39.000
UX Designer: Should we support multiple currencies? For international users?

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:46.000
Product Manager: Not for MVP. We'll just use dollars. Internationalization is post-MVP if there's demand.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:53.000
Developer: Any specific stores we need to support or is it just free-form text for store names?

00:05:53.500 --> 00:06:00.000
Product Manager: Free-form text is fine for MVP. Users can type any store name. Pre-populated store lists can come later.

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:07.000
Stakeholder 1: What about coupons? Can I track which coupons I have and factor those into prices?

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:14.000
Product Manager: Coupon management is a whole separate feature. Out of scope. Just tracking regular prices for now.

00:06:14.500 --> 00:06:21.000
UX Designer: Should we have favorites or frequently bought items highlighted?

00:06:21.500 --> 00:06:28.000
Product Manager: Nice UX enhancement but not critical. Post-MVP. For now, just alphabetical or creation order is fine.

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:35.000
Developer: What about export functionality? Like export to Excel or CSV?

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:42.000
Product Manager: Post-MVP. Good to have but not essential. Focus on core tracking and comparison first.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:49.000
Stakeholder 2: Can we get price drop notifications? Like an email when milk goes below $4?

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:56.000
Product Manager: That requires background jobs, email service, notification system. Way too complex for MVP. Out of scope.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:03.000
UX Designer: What about a quick add feature? Like voice input to add prices while shopping?

00:07:03.500 --> 00:07:10.000
Product Manager: Voice input is cool but definitely post-MVP. Requires speech recognition APIs and testing. Too advanced.

00:07:10.500 --> 00:07:17.000
Developer: Should we have tests? Unit tests, integration tests?

00:07:17.500 --> 00:07:24.000
Product Manager: Yes, some basic tests are part of MVP. At least for the API endpoints and main UI components.

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:31.000
Stakeholder 1: What about a shopping list generator? Like "make me a list of items where Store A has the best prices"?

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:38.000
Product Manager: That's an optimization feature with route planning. Out of scope. Users can view prices and decide themselves.

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:45.000
UX Designer: Should we have different views? Like a card view, list view, table view?

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:52.000
Product Manager: One view is enough for MVP. Keep it simple. Multiple view options are post-MVP.

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:59.000
Developer: Alright, let me summarize what I'm hearing for MVP. We need items CRUD, prices CRUD, price comparison with color coding, responsive design.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:06.000
Product Manager: Exactly. And optional notes field on prices. That's it. Everything else we discussed is either post-MVP or out of scope.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:13.000
Stakeholder 2: When can we expect to see the first version?

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:20.000
Product Manager: With this focused scope, we should have an MVP in about 2-3 days of focused development.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:27.000
Developer: Yeah, that's doable. React frontend, Node backend, SQLite database. Clean and simple.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:34.000
UX Designer: I'll work on wireframes today showing the item management and price comparison layouts.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:41.000
Product Manager: Perfect. Let's keep the scope tight and nail the core experience. We can always add features later based on feedback.

00:08:41.500 --> 00:08:48.000
Stakeholder 1: Sounds good. I'm excited to actually use this when it's ready!

00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:54.000
Product Manager: Great! Let's meet again in a couple days to review the MVP. Thanks everyone!